B.b4g=s(["Muscle naturally becomes harder to hold on to as we get older, and the drop in oestrogen around menopause can speed that up a little. Protein is one of the building blocks that helps.","Research suggests the body may make better use of protein when it is spread across the day, rather than mostly at dinner. A palm-sized portion at each meal is an easy rule of thumb.","Breakfast is often the lightest on protein. Greek yogurt, eggs, a glass of milk, or nut butter on toast are simple ways to give it a lift."],t.s)
B.bPC=new A.cL("protein-every-meal",B.km,"A little protein at every meal","Spreading protein across the day may help muscles more than one big serving.",B.b4g)
B.b5E=s(["Protein does not have to come from meat. Lentils, chickpeas, beans, tofu, tempeh and edamame all bring a good amount, along with fiber that animal foods do not have.","Mixing sources through the week, some animal and some plant, is a relaxed way to cover the bases."],t.s)
B.bPo=new A.cL("protein-plants",B.km,"Plant proteins count too","Beans, lentils, tofu and edamame bring protein plus fiber.",B.b5E)
B.bfm=s(["Eating protein on its own helps, but muscles respond most when they are also asked to work. Strength exercises, even body-weight ones like sit-to-stands or wall push-ups, give protein a job to do.","Having a protein-containing meal or snack in the hours after a session is a nice habit to pair with it."],t.s)
B.bPc=new A.cL("protein-strength",B.km,"Protein and movement work as a team","Muscles respond best when good food meets a bit of strength work.",B.bfm)
B.bfc=s(["Snacks that include some protein tend to be more satisfying and can help energy feel steadier.","A few ideas: yogurt with berries, a boiled egg, hummus with carrot sticks, a handful of nuts, cheese with an apple, or roasted chickpeas."],t.s)
B.bPb=new A.cL("protein-snacks",B.km,"Protein-friendly snacks","Small swaps that make snacks more filling.",B.bfc)
B.b4J=s(["Oestrogen plays a part in keeping bones strong. As levels fall during perimenopause and after menopause, bone can be lost more quickly than before.","Calcium, vitamin D, protein and weight-bearing movement all support bone health together. None of them has to be perfect every day; it is the overall pattern that counts.","If you have a family history of osteoporosis or have had a fracture, it is worth asking your doctor whether a bone health check makes sense for you."],t.s)
B.bPh=new A.cL("bones-why",B.kl,"Why bones need extra care now","Oestrogen helps protect bone, so its decline is a good moment to look after them.",B.b4J)
B.aRq=s(["Dairy foods such as milk, yogurt and cheese are well-known sources of calcium, but they are not the only ones.","Calcium-set tofu, tinned sardines or salmon (with the soft bones), kale, broccoli, bok choy, almonds, tahini, dried figs and fortified plant milks all contribute.","Recommended amounts vary by country, roughly 700 to 1,200 mg a day for women over 50. Your doctor can help you work out what is right for you, and whether a supplement is needed."],t.s)
B.bPs=new A.cL("bones-calcium-sources",B.kl,"Calcium beyond the dairy aisle","Tofu, tinned fish with bones, greens and fortified foods all help.",B.aRq)
B.b5R=s(["Vitamin D helps the body absorb and use calcium. Most of it is made in the skin from sunlight, so levels often dip in the darker months.","Oily fish, eggs, mushrooms that have been exposed to UV light, and fortified foods provide some, but it can be hard to get enough from food alone.","Many health bodies suggest considering a vitamin D supplement, especially in autumn and winter. It is a good thing to mention to your doctor or pharmacist, who can advise on what suits you."],t.s)
B.bPr=new A.cL("bones-vitamin-d",B.kl,"Vitamin D and the darker months","Sunlight is the main source, which is tricky in autumn and winter.",B.b5R)
B.b8q=s(["Bones get stronger when they carry load. Brisk walking, stair climbing, dancing, gentle jogging and strength exercises all count.","Balance work, such as standing on one leg while the kettle boils, or tai chi, helps reduce the chance of falls, which matters for bone health too."],t.s)
B.bPf=new A.cL("bones-move",B.kl,"Bones like a bit of impact","Walking, dancing and strength work help keep bones strong.",B.b8q)
B.b8I=s(["Fiber supports regular digestion, feeds friendly gut bacteria, helps keep cholesterol in check and can make energy feel more even through the day.","Many adults eat less than the commonly suggested 25 to 30 g a day. Small additions add up: oats at breakfast, a handful of beans in a soup, fruit with the skin on.","If you add a lot more fiber, do it gradually and drink plenty of water so your gut can adjust comfortably."],t.s)
B.bPq=new A.cL("fiber-why",B.kn,"Fiber, the quiet helper","Good for digestion, heart health and steadier energy.",B.b8I)
B.bs_=s(["Swapping white bread, rice or pasta for wholegrain versions is one of the simplest ways to eat more fiber.","Rye bread, oats, barley, brown rice, quinoa and wholewheat pasta are all good options. Half-and-half mixes are a gentle way to start if the taste is new."],t.s)
B.bPe=new A.cL("fiber-swaps",B.kn,"Easy wholegrain swaps","Brown, rye and wholemeal versions bring more fiber with little effort.",B.bs_)
B.b41=s(["A varied diet with lots of different plant foods seems to support a more diverse community of gut bacteria.","Fermented foods such as live yogurt, kefir, sauerkraut and kimchi are another way some people like to support gut health.","Some women notice more bloating or changes in digestion during perimenopause. If anything changes noticeably or persists, it is worth checking in with your doctor."],t.s)
B.bPp=new A.cL("fiber-gut",B.kn,"Feeding your gut","Variety and fermented foods may support a happy gut.",B.b41)
B.akT=s(["A spoonful of ground flaxseed, chia or mixed seeds stirred into porridge, yogurt or a smoothie adds fiber, healthy fats and a little protein.","Ground flaxseed is easier for the body to use than whole seeds. Keep it in the fridge to keep it fresh."],t.s)
B.bPn=new A.cL("fiber-seeds",B.kn,"A spoonful of seeds","Chia, flax and pumpkin seeds are a tiny addition with a lot to give.",B.akT)
B.bdi=s(["Different coloured fruits and vegetables carry different vitamins, minerals and antioxidants, so a mix of colours across the week is a lovely, easy goal.","Frozen and tinned fruit and veg count too, and are often just as nutritious as fresh."],t.s)
B.bPu=new A.cL("plants-colour",B.n9,"Eat the rainbow","Different colours bring different nutrients.",B.bdi)
B.aTI=s(["Adding a portion to something you already eat is often easier than planning something new. A handful of spinach in a sauce, berries on cereal, grated carrot in a sandwich, or an extra veg on the side.","Soups, stews, stir-fries and smoothies are great at carrying several portions at once."],t.s)
B.bPa=new A.cL("plants-five",B.n9,"Sneaking in an extra portion","Little additions to meals you already make.",B.aTI)
B.beR=s(["Soy foods such as tofu, edamame, tempeh and soy milk contain natural plant compounds called isoflavones. Some studies suggest they may modestly ease hot flushes for some women; others find little effect.","As part of a varied diet, soy foods are a good source of protein and, when fortified, calcium.","If you are considering soy or isoflavone supplements, especially with a history of breast cancer or if you take thyroid medication, please talk to your doctor first."],t.s)
B.bPB=new A.cL("plants-soy",B.n9,"Soy foods and menopause","Some women find soy foods helpful. The evidence is mixed.",B.beR)
B.bbu=s(["Oestrogen offers some protection to the heart and blood vessels, so after menopause it is a good time to look after heart health.","Eating patterns like the Mediterranean style, with plenty of vegetables, fruit, wholegrains, beans, nuts, olive oil and fish, are often linked with heart health.","Your doctor can check things like blood pressure and cholesterol, which is a helpful baseline to have."],t.s)
B.bPl=new A.cL("fats-heart",B.na,"Looking after your heart","Heart health becomes more important after menopause.",B.bbu)
B.brA=s(["Oily fish such as salmon, mackerel, sardines, trout and herring are rich in omega-3 fats, which are good for the heart and may support mood and brain health.","A common suggestion is around two portions of fish a week, one of them oily. Tinned fish counts and is very handy.","For plant sources, walnuts, chia seeds and ground flaxseed provide a different form of omega-3."],t.s)
B.bPA=new A.cL("fats-omega3",B.na,"Oily fish, twice a week","Salmon, mackerel and sardines bring omega-3 and vitamin D.",B.brA)
B.aGl=s(["Fat is not the enemy. Olive and rapeseed oils, nuts, seeds and avocado contain mostly unsaturated fats, which are a good choice for the heart.","A small handful of unsalted nuts a day is an easy habit that brings protein and fiber along too."],t.s)
B.bPk=new A.cL("fats-olive-oil",B.na,"Choosing friendlier fats","Olive oil, nuts, seeds and avocado are good everyday choices.",B.aGl)
B.bed=s(["Some women find that certain things set off hot flushes, such as caffeine, alcohol, spicy food or very hot drinks. Others notice no link at all.","Jotting down what you eat and drink alongside how you feel for a week or two can help you spot your own patterns. The food log is handy for this.","If hot flushes or night sweats are affecting your daily life, your doctor can talk you through the options available."],t.s)
B.bP9=new A.cL("comfort-triggers",B.tn,"Noticing your triggers","Caffeine, alcohol, spicy food and hot drinks bother some women.",B.bed)
B.b7w=s(["Sipping water through the day can help you feel more comfortable during hot flushes and supports energy, digestion and skin.","Keeping a glass or bottle within reach is often enough of a reminder. Cool drinks and water-rich foods such as cucumber, melon and soup all help."],t.s)
B.bPg=new A.cL("comfort-water",B.tn,"Keeping cool and hydrated","Water helps with hot flushes, energy and digestion.",B.b7w)
B.bgb=s(["Alcohol can make hot flushes and night sweats worse for some women, and tends to disturb sleep in the second half of the night.","It can also affect bone health and heart health over time. Having some alcohol-free days each week and enjoying smaller measures are kind steps to take."],t.s)
B.bPw=new A.cL("comfort-alcohol",B.tn,"A gentle look at alcohol","It can affect sleep, hot flushes and bone health.",B.bgb)
B.aJb=s(["Sleep can become more fragile during perimenopause. Caffeine stays in the body for several hours, so an afternoon coffee or tea can still be around at bedtime.","Trying a cut-off around midday or early afternoon, and switching to decaf or herbal teas later, is a simple experiment to see if it helps you."],t.s)
B.bPm=new A.cL("sleep-caffeine",B.AT,"Caffeine and a good night","An earlier cut-off may help sleep feel more restful.",B.aJb)
B.awX=s(["A very large or rich meal close to bedtime can make it harder to settle, and spicy food may bring on night sweats for some.","Leaving two to three hours between dinner and bed, where you can, may help. If you get hungry later, a small snack such as a yogurt or a banana is fine."],t.s)
B.bPx=new A.cL("sleep-evening-meal",B.AT,"Evening meals and sleep","A lighter, earlier dinner may be more comfortable.",B.awX)
B.b6t=s(["Meals that are mostly quick carbohydrates, like white toast and jam, can lead to an energy dip later on.","Pairing carbs with some protein, fiber or healthy fat, like toast with eggs, or fruit with nuts, tends to give steadier energy.","Regular meals help too. Going a long time without eating can leave you feeling tired and low."],t.s)
B.bPv=new A.cL("energy-steady",B.tk,"Steadier energy through the day","Pairing carbs with protein or fiber can help avoid the slump.",B.b6t)
B.bpu=s(["During perimenopause, periods can become irregular and sometimes heavier. Heavy periods can lower iron levels, which may leave you feeling tired.","Red meat, beans, lentils, tofu, dark green vegetables and fortified cereals all provide iron. Having vitamin C foods, such as peppers or citrus, at the same meal helps the body absorb iron from plants.","If your periods are heavy or you feel unusually tired, please see your doctor, who can check your iron levels."],t.s)
B.bPd=new A.cL("energy-iron",B.tk,"Iron and heavier periods","Periods can become heavier in perimenopause, which may lower iron.",B.bpu)
B.b4d=s(["Hormone changes can affect mood for some women. Food is not a fix, but regular meals, plenty of plants, and oily fish or other omega-3 sources are a kind foundation.","If low mood or anxiety is affecting your life, please reach out to your doctor. Support is available and you do not have to manage it alone."],t.s)
B.bPj=new A.cL("energy-mood",B.tk,"Food and mood","Regular, varied meals can be one small support for mood.",B.b4d)
B.b5x=s(["Many health guidelines suggest around 150 minutes of moderate activity a week, such as brisk walking, cycling or dancing. That could be 30 minutes on five days, or shorter bursts added together.","Moderate means you are a little warm and breathing faster, but can still chat. A 10-minute walk after meals is an easy way to build it up and may help energy feel steadier.","If you have a health condition or are starting something new, it is a good idea to check with your doctor first."],t.s)
B.bPi=new A.cL("move-150",B.tl,"Moving more, gently","About 150 minutes a week is a common guideline. Every bit counts.",B.b5x)
B.b_y=s(["Guidelines often suggest strength exercises on at least two days a week. Around menopause this matters more, because muscle and bone are easier to lose.","It does not need a gym. Sit-to-stands from a chair, wall push-ups, step-ups on the stairs, or carrying shopping bags all count. Resistance bands and light weights are a nice next step.","Start gently, focus on good form, and build up slowly. A physiotherapist or your doctor can help if you have joint pain or other concerns."],t.s)
B.bPt=new A.cL("move-strength",B.tl,"Strength work, twice a week","Simple strength moves help protect muscles and bones.",B.b_y)
B.b46=s(["Regular activity is linked with better mood, less stress and more restful sleep for many people, which can be especially welcome during perimenopause.","Morning or daytime activity, ideally outdoors in daylight, tends to be kindest to sleep. Very vigorous exercise late in the evening keeps some people awake.","Gentle options like yoga, tai chi or a slow walk are just as valuable on days when energy is low."],t.s)
B.bPz=new A.cL("move-mood-sleep",B.tl,"Movement for mood and sleep","Being active may help mood, stress and sleep.",B.b46)
B.bhp=s(["This app is here to help you notice, not to judge. Some days will be colourful and protein-rich, and some will be toast on the sofa. Both are fine.","Looking at the pattern over a week is often more useful, and much kinder, than worrying about a single day."],t.s)
B.bPy=new A.cL("everyday-gentle",B.tm,"Progress, not perfection","Every day does not have to tick every box.",B.bhp)
B.bkf=s(["Most salt comes from processed foods such as bread, cured meats, ready meals and sauces, rather than the salt shaker.","Herbs, spices, lemon and garlic are great ways to add flavour. Tasting before adding salt is a simple habit."],t.s)
B.bPE=new A.cL("everyday-salt",B.tm,"Easy on the salt","Less salt is kinder to blood pressure, and may help bones too.",B.bkf)
B.b8K=s(["Everyone's experience of perimenopause and menopause is different. What works for one person may not suit another.","Before making big changes to your diet, starting supplements, or if something does not feel right, please talk to your doctor. They can look at your own health history and help you find what works for you."],t.s)
B.bPD=new A.cL("everyday-doctor",B.tm,"Your doctor is on your team","Perimenopause is individual, and support is available.",B.b8K)
B.jG=s([B.bPC,B.bPo,B.bPc,B.bPb,B.bPh,B.bPs,B.bPr,B.bPf,B.bPq,B.bPe,B.bPp,B.bPn,B.bPu,B.bPa,B.bPB,B.bPl,B.bPA,B.bPk,B.bP9,B.bPg,B.bPw,B.bPm,B.bPx,B.bPv,B.bPd,B.bPj,B.bPi,B.bPt,B.bPz,B.bPy,B.bPE,B.bPD],t.dp)
